                       "initiated the alluvial method"))
  )
Description: Organises the study of networks built from tabular research
    data (articles and the references they cite, authors and their papers,
    accounts and what they share) as a sequence of explicit steps. It
    projects two-mode data (bibliographic coupling, co-citation, coupling of
    authors, co-authorship...) onto one-mode networks, weighted by
    bibliometric similarity measures or filtered by statistical backbones,
    for a whole corpus or one network per time window; detects communities with the
    'igraph' algorithms (Leiden, Louvain, Infomap...); links the communities
    of successive windows into dynamic clusters; names them with tf-idf or
    central nodes; characterises the roles of nodes; and draws networks and
    alluvial plots. Networks are 'tidygraph' objects.
